  "text": "The ability of several synthetic filter sheets to block salt was tested under high pressure. The filters were grouped into two types: those that resist the passage of both positively and negatively charged ions, and those that mainly block the positively charged ions. Materials that contain water‑attracting groups and show strong resistance to both ion types were placed in the first group. Polymers that carry fixed negative charges and therefore hinder only the positive ions fell into the second group. In addition to a previously studied acetate‑based polymer, a butyrate‑modified cellulose, a vinyl‑ketone polymer supported on a thin film, and a vinyl‑acetate polymer on the same support all behaved as the first type. The unsupported vinyl‑ketone and vinyl‑acetate polymers alone did not stop salt; the thin film support limited the spacing between polymer chains at the surface. Blends of a 1:1 copolymer containing acidic groups with a water‑soluble polymer formed robust, ion‑selective filters. Heat‑treating these blends reduced swelling, lowered flow, and improved salt rejection. When the copolymer content was high, the sheets became fragile and needed reinforcement with a supporting film to achieve good rejection. Experiments showed that for both filter types, high amounts of the functional groups and tight chain packing are essential; smaller pores give better separation but also reduce flow."
}
